<section class="faq-section" id="faq">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="section-kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do I choose commercial LED lighting for a warehouse?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Start with mounting height and layout. High ceilings and open floors typically call for UFO high bay lights, while long aisles with racking benefit from linear high bay lights for directional coverage.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What type of LED lighting is best for an office?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Panel lights are the standard choice for suspended-ceiling offices needing uniform, low-glare light. Tube lights make sense when you're retrofitting existing fluorescent troffers rather than replacing the fixture entirely.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What's the difference between UFO and linear high bay lights?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>UFO high bays have a compact, round form factor that distributes light broadly across open floor areas. Linear high bays are rectangular and distribute light in a directional pattern, which works well along aisles and racking rows.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What should I consider when choosing outdoor commercial lighting?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Environmental fit matters most: confirm the wet/damp location rating for the mounting location, choose a beam pattern that matches the area's shape, and plan controls (photocell or motion) to manage operating hours.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do I know whether a commercial fixture will work with my existing electrical system?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Confirm the building's voltage (commonly 120V, 277V, or universal input) and, for retrofits, verify ballast compatibility or wiring method before ordering. When in doubt, check with your electrician before finalizing fixture selection.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Do commercial lighting projects require DLC-listed fixtures?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Not always — but DLC listing is often required for utility rebate eligibility. If your project may qualify for a rebate program, verify DLC status for the specific product against current program requirements.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What should I check before ordering commercial LED fixtures?<span class="icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Confirm application, fixture type, dimensions, mounting method and height, light distribution, CCT/CRI, voltage, controls, environmental rating, safety listing, and any applicable code or rebate requirements. See the full checklist above.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

